      en: [
        { type: "h2", content: "What does statutory insurance approval mean - and why does it matter?" },
        { type: "p", content: "Statutory insurance approval (Kassensitz) is a therapist's authorisation to bill statutory health insurers directly. Without it, treatment must either be paid privately or applied for through a lengthy cost-reimbursement process with the insurer - with an uncertain outcome. With statutory approval, billing goes directly through the insurer. For families this means: no out-of-pocket costs, no upfront payment, no extra cost coverage that first has to be applied for." },
        { type: "highlight", content: "In Germany, children and young people often wait several months for a therapy place - in some regions considerably longer. A dedicated statutory approval with in-house capacity is one of the few ways to meaningfully shorten that wait." },
        { type: "h2", content: "How long are waiting times normally - and how does it work at Ankernetz?" },
        { type: "p", content: "Long waiting lists are caused by a structural shortage of statutory approvals for child and adolescent psychotherapy in many regions of Germany. Ankernetz addresses this with its own statutory approval and in-house capacity - removing the need for an external waiting list. Children already cared for in an Ankernetz facility receive priority access, but outpatient children without residential placement can also apply." },
        { type: "h2", content: "Who is the therapy for?" },
        { type: "p", content: "The service is aimed at children and young people from around age 5. Younger children who are not yet communicating verbally can also be treated - here symbolic play, sand play and other creative media are used as a therapeutic approach, rather than relying on language." },
        { type: "h2", content: "Which methods are used?" },
        { type: "ul", content: ["Play therapy approaches - especially for younger children", "Behavioural analysis and behaviour-therapy methods", "Skills training to build coping strategies", "Symbolic play and sand play for non-verbal or very young children", "Attachment-focused methods for attachment disorders and disorganised attachment patterns"] },
        { type: "p", content: "Parents are always involved - child psychotherapy does not take place in isolation from the family environment but in close coordination with parents or guardians." },
        { type: "h2", content: "How does cost coverage work?" },
        { type: "p", content: "With statutory approval, families do not need to submit a separate application - treatment is billed directly through all statutory health insurers. Where an application for cost coverage is still needed, for example for certain additional services, the Ankernetz team handles the paperwork - for families and youth welfare offices alike. The team usually responds to an enquiry within 24 hours." },
        { type: "h2", content: "Short-term or long-term therapy - what is the difference?" },
        { type: "p", content: "Depending on assessment and need, a distinction is made between short-term therapy with 12 to 24 sessions and long-term therapy with up to 80 sessions. Which form fits emerges from the initial diagnostic conversations - not from a rigid rule. The goal is always to offer as much therapy as needed, without unnecessarily long courses of treatment." },
        { type: "h2", content: "What does a typical therapy session look like?" },
        { type: "p", content: "A therapy session with younger children often takes a playful form - through role play, drawing, sand play or other creative approaches that let the child express themselves without having to put everything into words. With older children and young people, conversation moves more to the centre, complemented by concrete exercises for dealing with feelings, conflicts or distressing memories. Regular conversations with parents accompany the process." },
        { type: "h2", content: "When should parents consider professional help for their child?" },
        { type: "ul", content: ["Persistent anxiety that noticeably restricts daily life", "Marked changes in behaviour with no obvious external trigger", "Withdrawal from friends, school or previous interests", "Sleep problems, nightmares or physical complaints with no medical cause", "Difficulties following distressing events such as separation, loss or violence", "Self-harming behaviour or suicidal thoughts - immediate action is important here"] },
        { type: "h2", content: "Ankernetz: psychotherapy with short paths" },
        { type: "p", content: "Ankernetz combines its own statutory approval with a connected support system - diagnostics, counselling and, if needed, inpatient support are all available under one roof. For families this means no long detours between practices and offices, but a single point of contact who accompanies the whole process." },
      ],
